  "analysis": "The problem is Codeforces 196B Infinite Maze. We need to determine if, in an infinite tiling of the given n x m grid (with walls '#', passable '.', and a start 'S'), the boy can walk infinitely far from the start. A standard approach is to perform a search on the infinite grid but avoid exploring infinitely many tiles by tracking, for each base cell, the different tile offsets (tile coordinates) from which that cell has been reached. If any base cell is reached from two distinct tile offsets, then an infinite path exists (you can repeat the offset difference to go to infinity). We implement a BFS/DFS-like traversal on the implicit infinite grid, but store at most two tile offsets per base cell. When a second distinct offset for a cell is discovered, we can answer Yes immediately. If the search completes without finding two offsets for any cell, the answer is No. This approach is O(2 * n * m) states in the worst case and fits within memory/time constraints for n, m up to 1500.\n\nPlan to implement:\n- Parse input, locate the start S, and treat it as passable.\n- Use BFS-like queue storing (cell_index, tile_x, tile_y).\n- For each move, handle wrap-around when crossing tile boundaries: moving off the top/bottom/left/right wraps to the adjacent tile, adjusting tile coordinates accordingly.\n- Maintain per-cell storage of up to two tile offsets (t1 and t2). If a new offset for a cell is encountered and it’s not already stored, add it. If a second distinct offset is added for a cell, print Yes and exit.\n- If the queue empties without finding two offsets for any cell, print No.\n\nI’ll implement this in C++17 for performance and memory efficiency, using flat indexing r*m + c for cells and arrays t1x/t1y/t2x/t2y along with a small cnt array to track how many offsets are stored per cell.",
  "plan": "Next steps:\n- Write a C++17 solution implementing the described BFS with per-cell two-offset tracking.\n- Compile the program.\n- Run it on the provided sample tests to verify correctness.\n- Report the results (Yes/No for each sample).",
